  "stations": [
    {
      "id": "cafe-counter",
      "type": "work",
      "region": [
        106,
        154,
        248,
        224
      ],
      "anchor": [
        176.5,
        197.0
      ],
      "facingDeg": 78.2,
      "walkable": false,
      "anchorNote": "She stands on the shop floor behind the counter. y=178 had her feet on the counter's own top surface, which is what the owner spotted; y=160 before that sat almost on the horizon and rendered her far too small.",
      "clipY": 173.0,
      "clipNote": "The counter stands between the keeper and the camera and hides her lower body. It is not painted into any occluder layer - the owner deferred that, and a flat mask would have been wrong anyway, since how much of a body the counter hides depends on where that body stands. So the keeper alone is cut at a stated row: clipY is the counter's top edge as it projects at her column, and her anchor is the shop floor she stands on behind it. Two numbers, both adjustable by eye. If anyone ever walks behind that counter, this stops being enough and the counter front has to be painted."
    }
  ],
  "seatAnchorNote": "frontEdge is the front lip of the seat surface, drawn directly on the scene by the owner and read back off the image. A seated sprite is placed so its knee covers that point.\n\nEverything that used to derive this is gone. Deriving it needed the chair's floor line, its seat height and which side the occupant faced, and each of those was wrong in turn: the painted blobs stop short of the chair legs, seat heights differ between stools and chairs, and the lip moves to the far side when the sitter faces away. Eleven drawn lines settled all of it.",
  "facingNote": "facingDeg comes from what the seat is drawn up to, not from the painted chair back. A table chair faces its table, a counter stool faces the counter, the park bench faces the open ground. Two attempts at reading it off the chair-back paint both failed: the back is an upright panel, so its centroid sits above its seat and reported every chair as facing the camera, and moving to the back's bottom edge fixed the sign but not the spread, because a hand-painted patch a few pixels across cannot pin an angle. The table is the better instrument - every seat around it agrees on where it is. backrestTopY is the top row of the nearest painted back; the renderer uses it as that back's depth so a chair does not cover its own occupant, which is a deliberate stopgap - some backs really do stand in front of their sitter, and occlusion is deferred."
}